  "version": "other",
  "operative_text": "Sec 1 new Art 54C: carry concealed handgun without permit (ON-TOPIC, permitless carry): Any person who is a citizen of the United States and is at least 18 years old may carry a concealed handgun in this State unless provided otherwise by law.\nSec 1 14-415.35(b)-(d): prohibited persons, ID and disclosure duty, penalties: When carrying a concealed handgun, a person shall also carry valid identification and shall disclose to any law enforcement officer that the person is carrying a concealed handgun\nSec 1 14-415.36: places where concealed carry is unlawful: It is unlawful to carry a concealed weapon into the following areas unless provided otherwise by law:\nSec 1.9 line-of-duty death benefit raised: the Industrial Commission shall award a death benefit in the amount of one hundred thousand dollars ($100,000) one hundred and fifty thousand dollars ($150,000)\nSec 2 permit continues for reciprocity: the State of North Carolina shall continue to make a concealed handgun permit available to any person who applies for and is eligible to receive a concealed handgun permit pursuant to this Article.\nSec 3 14-415.12(b) permit denial grounds amended: The sheriff shall deny a permit to an applicant who:\nSec 4 14-269 carrying concealed weapons conformed: Except as otherwise provided by law, it is unlawful for any person willfully and intentionally to carry concealed about his or her person any bowie knife\nSec 5 14-269.1 confiscation conformed: Upon conviction of any person for violation of G.S. 14-269, G.S. 14-269.7, 14-269.7, 14-415.35(b)\nSec 6 14-269.3 weapons into alcohol assemblies: It shall be unlawful for any person consuming alcohol, or at any time while the person has remaining in the person's body any alcohol\nSec 7 14-269.4 weapons on State property and courthouses: It shall be unlawful for any person to possess, or carry, whether openly or concealed, any deadly weapon\nSec 8 14-277.2 parades: The provisions of this section shall not apply to concealed carry of a handgun at a parade or funeral procession by a person with a valid permit\nSec 9 14-401.24 drones definition conformed: Weapon. – Those weapons specified in G.S. 14-269, 14-269.2, 14-284.1, or\nSec 9.1(a) 14-34.5 assault with a firearm on law enforcement, probation, parole, National Guard, detention staff: Any person who commits an assault with a firearm upon a law enforcement officer, probation officer, or parole officer while the officer is in the performance of his or her duties is\nSec 9.1(b) 14-34.6(c) assault with a firearm on emergency, medical personnel: a person is guilty of a Class D felony if the person violates subsection (a) of this section against a person not otherwise covered under G.S. 14-34.5 and uses a firearm.\nSec 10 14-409.40 local regulation (preemption) conformed: Nothing contained in this section prohibits municipalities or counties from application of their authority under G.S. 153A-129, 160A-189\nSec 11 14-415.4(e)(2) firearm-rights restoration: The petitioner is under indictment for a felony or a finding of probable cause exists against the petitioner for a felony.\nSec 12 14-415.11(a) permit holders carry: Any person who has a concealed handgun permit may carry a concealed handgun unless otherwise specifically prohibited by law.\nSec 13 repeal of 14-415.22: G.S. 14-415.22 is repealed.\nSec 14 74E-6 company police: Company police officers, while in the performance of their duties of employment, have the same powers as municipal and county police officers\nSec 15 74G-6 campus police concealed weapons: Campus police officers shall have, if duly authorized by their campus police agency and by the sheriff of the county\nSec 15.5 discharge by felon during felony: A person who violates subsection (a) of this section and discharges a firearm during the commission or attempted commission of a felony\nSec 15.6 14-415.1 felon in possession amended: It shall be is unlawful for any person who has been convicted of a felony to purchase, own, possess, or have in his the person's custody, care, or control any firearm\nSec 16 113-136 wildlife inspector enforcement: Enforcement authority of inspectors and protectors; refusal to obey or allow inspection by inspectors and protectors.\nSec 16.7 UNC scholarship for children of disabled or slain first responders: The University of North Carolina Board of Governors shall develop a scholarship program for any child, if the child is at least 17 years old but not yet 28 years old\nSec 17-18 savings and effective date: This act becomes effective December 1, 2025, and applies to offenses committed on or after that date.",
